/* Only the ffdas C API leaves this library.
 *
 * ffdas links the C++ runtime statically (see its CMakeLists.txt) so it stays
 * self-contained and cannot clash with the runtime the host already has -- under
 * MATLAB, which ships its own, or under CPython. Static, though, does not mean
 * private: without a version script the linker puts every one of those runtime
 * symbols in the dynamic symbol table, and the loader then binds the *whole
 * process* -- the echoframe extension, the CLI, anything else -- to this second,
 * private copy of the standard library.
 *
 * Two copies do not agree. Each has its own std::locale::id table, so a
 * std::use_facet<> lookup keyed on the host's id misses the facet the host
 * registered, throws std::bad_cast, and the stream it was called on is marked
 * bad. That is silent by default: with exceptions off on std::cout the first
 * std::cout << <arithmetic> leaves the stream in an error state and every later
 * write to it is discarded. The banners stop mid-line, partway through the first
 * GPU-info row, and only once something else in the process has perturbed the
 * lookup order -- importing matplotlib from Python is enough, which is why it
 * reads as a matplotlib bug and why echoframe_cli on the same machine looks fine.
 *
 * `local: *` is what makes the static link private. It applies to defined
 * symbols only, so the undefined CUDA runtime entry points ffdas still imports
 * resolve exactly as before.
 */
FFDAS_1.0 {
  global:
    ffdas_*;
  local:
    *;
};
